schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Inclusion does not mean endorsement"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Adding a source to a knowledge commons means it is "relevant enough to
    preserve, classify, review, route, or revisit" — it does not mean the
    project vouches for or endorses its content.
  evidence: >-
    Stated as an explicit Toolkit rule in its own reference docs [RKC],
    paired with the practice of recording provenance on everything so raw,
    unverified material can be kept without quietly becoming treated as true.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/source-systems.md ([RKC] reference)
  interpretation: >-
    This is the mechanical justification for why provenance and honest
    maturity labelling matter — without them, "inclusion ≠ endorsement"
    cannot actually hold, because readers have no way to tell reviewed
    material from raw inclusions.
  uncertainty: >-
    Sourced to the toolkit's own internal reference docs via a generic
    placeholder URL in this article's references list — verify the actual
    citation target before treating this as an external, independently
    reviewed claim.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/source-systems.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
